          "quoted_text": "Intentionally launching an attack in the knowledge that such attack will cause incidental loss of life or injury to civilians or damage to civilian objects or widespread, long-term and severe damage to the natural environment which would be clearly excessive in relation to the concrete and direct overall military advantage anticipated"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Even in an extreme self‑defence scenario, the use of nuclear weapons is prohibited because it would destroy objects indispensable to the survival of the civilian population, which Additional Protocol I article 54 forbids except for a narrow derogation that does not apply to nuclear weapons.",
      "reasoning": "Article 54 of Additional Protocol I expressly bans attacks on objects indispensable to civilian survival—such as food, water, agricultural land and livestock—unless the destruction occurs within the State’s own territory and is justified by imperative military necessity. Nuclear weapons cannot be confined to a limited area; their blast, heat and radiation inevitably affect indispensable civilian objects across borders, and the limited derogation does not extend to such trans‑boundary, indiscriminate effects. Consequently, their use breaches the prohibition on attacking indispensable civilian objects.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "54",
          "quoted_text": "It is prohibited to attack, destroy, remove or render useless objects indispensable to the survival of the civilian population, such as foodstuffs, agricultural areas for the production of foodstuffs, crops, livestock, drinking water installations and supplies and irrigation works, for the specific purpose of denying them for their sustenance value to the civilian population or to the adverse Party, whatever the motive, whether in order to starve out civilians, to cause them move away, or for any other motive."
